 in touch  as well he says who decides whether a  school should remain open and at what  temperature is there a legal requirement  for them to close if indeed there is  i think that's the obligation of the  head teacher that they're able to make  assessment on the ground in the context  that they are given uh from one part of  the country to another there could be  quite uh significant changes in the  temperature it's something that i'm  looking at every hour here today with  we're looking at a temperature that goes  up towards 40 degrees here tomorrow  we've got to make sure that it's safe  for young people and as a head teacher  that's your first priority so it's the  head teacher on the ground that will  make that decision  and uh miriam how do we prepare for the  future if we're going to get more days  like this if we're going to get extended  periods of really hot weather what does  the nhs need to do to adapt  i think events like this really show  that uh capacity in the nhs hasn't kept  pace uh with changing population need  and how the world's evolved so we really  need to work with government i think to  look at nhs funding and make sure we've  got enough capacity across all of our  services so certainly in the ambulance  sector and the acute hospital sector  which you've talked about but also  across community and mental health  services and today really does point to  a pressing need to look at the nhs  estate and really to release as much  capital as possible to modernize some of  the buildings that we're we're dealing  with and trying to provide care within a  lot of those buildings are quite  difficult to manage in normal uk  temperatures and days like today just  really show the acute need to invest in  them more and bring them up to modern  day standards  okay miriam deakin uh from nhs providers  kevin groves from network rail and pepe  diazio head teacher of wales high school  in rotherham matt great to get your  thoughts and comments and answering 